<p>Our lab is involved with the development of several techniques to assist with the accurate identification and measurement of various neural structures&#8230;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Probabilistic tractography group analysis using cytoarchitectonic ROIs of human nuclei</p>
<p><a href="/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/pic3.png"><img class="size-full wp-image-202 alignnone" alt="pic3" src="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/pic3.png" width="303" height="361" srcset="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/pic3.png 303w, https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/pic3-251x300.png 251w" sizes="(max-width: 303px) 100vw, 303px"/></a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>As technology improves, more accurate image feature measurement techniques will be available to help identify stronger and more robust radio-genomic correlations which will lead to insights about cancer biology. Our lab has developed a semi-automatic in silico measurement pipeline to segment and interpolate the volume of various tumor compartments using a series of two dimensional MR images.</p>
<p><a href="/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/mrianalys.png"><img class="size-full wp-image-368 alignnone" alt="mrianalys" src="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/mrianalys.png" width="913" height="165" srcset="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/mrianalys.png 913w, https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/10/mrianalys-300x54.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 913px) 100vw, 913px"/></a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>FSL: Our lab incorporates several tools available from the FSL toolset, a subset of the FMRIB Software Library hosted by Oxford University. FSL is a comprehensive library of analysis tools for FMRI, MRI and DTI brain imaging data that can be run on both Apple and PCs (For more information click <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20200222233948/http://fsl.fmrib.ox.ac.uk/fsl/fslwiki/">here</a>)</p>
<p><a href="/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/01/FSL-GUIs.png"><img class="size-large wp-image-396 alignnone" alt="FSL-GUIs" src="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/01/FSL-GUIs-1024x641.png" width="391" height="244" srcset="https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/01/FSL-GUIs-1024x641.png 1024w, https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/01/FSL-GUIs-300x188.png 300w, https://web.archive.org/web/20200224105019im_/http://drgutman.org/dglab_wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2013/01/FSL-GUIs.png 1088w" sizes="(max-width: 391px) 100vw, 391px"/></a></p>
